---
export const title = 'Virtual scroll';
export const order = 520;
import DemoComponentCard from '../../../../components/DemoComponentCard.astro';
---

<p>
  Set <code>virtual</code> to show a large data set without pagination: the table renders only the
  rows in view, plus a few on each side, between two spacers that keep the scroll height of every
  row. Give the table a height, so it scrolls inside itself.
</p>
<p>
  Every row has the same, fixed height: <code>--table-row-height</code> (set by
  <code>density</code>). Rows of different heights are not supported, so cells do not wrap.
  <code>virtual</code> has no effect while rows have details (<code>detailTemplate</code>); grouped
  rows work, because a group header is one row high too.
</p>
<p>
  <code>aria-rowcount</code> and each row's <code>aria-rowindex</code> describe the whole data set.
  The arrow keys, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>End</kbd> move through
  every row: the table scrolls the target row into view first. When the active row scrolls out of
  view, the first rendered row takes the tab stop.
</p>

<DemoComponentCard lang='html'>
  <div style='width: 100%; height: 24rem;'>
    <wc-table id='table-virtual' virtual label='10,000 rows'></wc-table>
  </div>

  <script is:inline>
    const $table = document.getElementById('table-virtual');
    $table.columns = [
      { name: 'id', label: 'ID', width: 7, type: 'number' },
      { name: 'name', label: 'Name', width: 14 },
      { name: 'score', label: 'Score', width: 8, type: 'number' },
    ];
    $table.data = Array.from({ length: 10000 }, (_, index) => ({
      id: index + 1,
      name: `Item ${index + 1}`,
      score: (index * 37) % 100,
    }));
  </script>
</DemoComponentCard>
